{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjXJ7JycNiFxxXbRe62Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019ZguRfsfEPCr5TSbeHNRFC","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start: the clef, then a bar holding only a whole-bar rest mark (the dot/rest on the staff). The edition's bar 33 is a full-bar rest, so this is bar 33. The \"dol.\" written above it is not in the edition's text for that bar; it may belong to the line above. After the first barline comes \"rinfor.\" and a dotted half on D4, just below the staff. That matches bar 34's [\"rinf\"] (D4 half. The slur starting there runs into the next bar. Next bar: a single Eb4 eighth, where the slur ends, then a beamed group of Eb4, Eb4, Eb4, D4, D4. That is six eighths, Eb Eb Eb Eb D D, which matches bar 35. After the last barline there is one Eb4 quarter at the right edge. That is the start of bar 36 (Eb4 quarter), and its two quarter rests must be on the next line. So the line runs from bar 33 to the start of bar 36. The only mismatch is the extra \"dol.\" marking.","right":true,"insights":"The line was easy to match. The rinf. dotted half on D4 and the run of Eb eighths falling to D are distinctive, and they fix bars 34–35 without doubt. The hard parts were at the edges: a full-bar rest could be any of several silent bars, and the line ends with a lone quarter. Placing the line depended on its neighbours. Clearer barlines at the edges, or the next line's opening, would have made the end of bar 36 easier to confirm."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4999,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4999},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":